.Dd October 8, 2026
.Dt V4L2_STATELESS 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm v4l2_stateless
.Nd the stateless video decoder interface of the freebsd-media V4L2 stack
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This page tells a programmer of a decoder client what the interface of
.Xr cedrus 4
(on the
.Xr media 4
core) is, and where it differs from what other decoders do.
The interface is that of the Linux
.Dq stateless decoder
documentation; headers are in
.Pa sys/dev/media/uapi/linux .
FFmpeg's V4L2 request hwaccel (Kwiboo's
.Dq v4l2-request
branch) is a working client.
.Ss Model
The decoder keeps no state between pictures: the client parses the bit stream, and for each slice
sends the slice data and the parameters.
There are two queues on
.Pa /dev/videoN :
.Bl -tag -width ".Dv V4L2_BUF_TYPE_VIDEO_CAPTURE"
.It Dv V4L2_BUF_TYPE_VIDEO_OUTPUT
The coded data, pixel format
.Dv V4L2_PIX_FMT_H264_SLICE
or
.Dv V4L2_PIX_FMT_MPEG2_SLICE .
.It Dv V4L2_BUF_TYPE_VIDEO_CAPTURE
The decoded picture, pixel format
.Dv V4L2_PIX_FMT_NV12 .
.El
.Pp
Parameters are controls set
.Em in a media request :
allocate a request with
.Dv MEDIA_IOC_REQUEST_ALLOC
on
.Pa /dev/mediaN ,
set the controls with
.Dv VIDIOC_S_EXT_CTRLS
.Pq Va which No = Dv V4L2_CTRL_WHICH_REQUEST_VAL , Va request_fd No = the request ,
queue the OUTPUT buffer with
.Dv V4L2_BUF_FLAG_REQUEST_FD
and the same request, and then
.Dv MEDIA_REQUEST_IOC_QUEUE .
Wait for the request with
.Xr poll 2
.Pq Dv POLLPRI
or for the buffers.
The CAPTURE buffer that receives the picture is the next one queued; its
.Va timestamp
is set to the OUTPUT buffer's, and a picture that is a reference for a later one is named by that
timestamp.
.Pp
Set the format on the OUTPUT queue first (the pixel format and the picture size, the latter is
clamped to 16 to 2048 per dimension; the buffer size is at least 64 KiB, at most 32 MiB), then
read the CAPTURE format back: the driver derives it from the OUTPUT format.
CAPTURE planes are NV12 with the line stride rounded up to 32 pixels and the height rounded up to
a multiple of 16.
.Pp
The decoded picture can be exported with
.Dv VIDIOC_EXPBUF
as a
.Xr dmabuf 4
descriptor.
When it is mapped with
.Xr mmap 2 ,
bracket the access with
.Dv DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC .
The memory is uncached: reading it from the CPU is slow, copy with large reads or do not read it.
.Ss Supported formats
.Bl -column "V4L2_PIX_FMT_MPEG2_SLICE" "Queue" "Notes" -offset indent
.It Sy "Format" Ta Sy "Queue" Ta Sy "Notes"
.It Dv V4L2_PIX_FMT_H264_SLICE Ta OUTPUT Ta Annex B start codes, slice based
.It Dv V4L2_PIX_FMT_MPEG2_SLICE Ta OUTPUT Ta one picture per buffer
.It Dv V4L2_PIX_FMT_NV12 Ta CAPTURE Ta 8 bit 4:2:0
.El
.Ss Controls: H.264
All are set in each request.
.Bl -column "V4L2_CID_STATELESS_H264_PRED_WEIGHTS" "Notes" -offset indent
.It Sy "Control" Ta Sy "Notes"
.It Dv V4L2_CID_STATELESS_H264_SPS Ta
.It Dv V4L2_CID_STATELESS_H264_PPS Ta slice groups (FMO) are refused
.It Dv V4L2_CID_STATELESS_H264_SCALING_MATRIX Ta
.It Dv V4L2_CID_STATELESS_H264_PRED_WEIGHTS Ta used if the slice needs it
.It Dv V4L2_CID_STATELESS_H264_SLICE_PARAMS Ta exactly one slice per request
.It Dv V4L2_CID_STATELESS_H264_DECODE_PARAMS Ta DPB entries name references by timestamp
.It Dv V4L2_CID_STATELESS_H264_DECODE_MODE Ta only SLICE_BASED
.It Dv V4L2_CID_STATELESS_H264_START_CODE Ta only ANNEX_B
.El
.Pp
Refused with
.Er ENOTSUP
(the request completes with an error): more than one slice in
.Dv SLICE_PARAMS ,
bit depth above 8, chroma_format_idc above 1, separate colour planes, SP and SI slices, slice
groups.
.Ss Controls: MPEG-2
.Bl -tag -width ".Dv V4L2_CID_STATELESS_MPEG2_QUANTISATION" -offset indent
.It Dv V4L2_CID_STATELESS_MPEG2_SEQUENCE
.It Dv V4L2_CID_STATELESS_MPEG2_PICTURE
Names the forward and backward reference pictures by the timestamp of the CAPTURE buffer holding
them.
.It Dv V4L2_CID_STATELESS_MPEG2_QUANTISATION
.El
.Pp
The OUTPUT buffer starts at, or somewhere before, the first slice start code of the picture.
.Ss Slice data
The OUTPUT buffer of an H.264 request holds one NAL unit: the slice's Annex B start code and the NAL
unit, ending where the NAL unit ends.
.Sy header_bit_size
(in
.Vt struct v4l2_ctrl_h264_slice_params )
is, in this driver, given in
.Em FFmpeg's convention :
the number of bits from the first bit of the NAL header byte to the start of slice_data(), counted in
the RBSP, so without the start code and without emulation prevention bytes.
The driver adds the start code (24 or 32 bits) itself.
A client that counts from the first byte of the buffer, start code included, sets
.Va dev.cedrus.0.h264_hdrmode
to 0.
.Pp
Trailing zero bytes of the NAL unit (the zero_byte of the next four-byte start code when the NAL
was cut at the next start code) are removed by the driver.
.Ss Fields and several slices per picture: HOLD_CAPTURE_BUF
All slices of one picture, and both fields of a frame coded as two field pictures, go to
.Em one
CAPTURE buffer.
Every OUTPUT buffer of the picture except the last carries
.Dv V4L2_BUF_FLAG_M2M_HOLD_CAPTURE_BUF
(the queue reports
.Dv V4L2_BUF_CAP_SUPPORTS_M2M_HOLD_CAPTURE_BUF ) .
The CAPTURE buffer is held until a source buffer without the flag completes, then returned.
For fields, the first field's source buffer is flagged and the second field's is not.
The held field is a reference of the second field.
If one slice fails, the rest of the held slices complete with an error and the capture buffer is
returned flagged as an error.
.Ss Reference pictures
A reference is named by the
.Va timestamp
of the CAPTURE buffer that holds it
.Pq Va reference_ts No in the DPB entries, Va forward_ref_ts No and Va backward_ref_ts No for MPEG-2 .
The buffer must not be queued again, and its timestamp must stay unchanged, while it is a
reference.
A reference that is not found (or not usable by the engine) counts in
.Va dev.cedrus.0.missing_refs ;
the picture's own destination buffer is used in its place.
.Pp
The driver keeps a table slot per CAPTURE buffer for as long as the buffer is in the DPB, so the
DPB entry order a client uses (FFmpeg numbers them by recency) does not matter.
.Ss What comes out
H.264: bit-identical to a software decoder (tested on the 173 supported streams of the JVT
conformance suite, through the driver and through FFmpeg).
MPEG-2: within two sample values per sample of a software decoder (the inverse transform is not
bit-exact).
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cedrus 4 ,
.Xr media 4 ,
.Xr dmabuf 4
